# The desktop's own PAM stack, and the only reason it has one.
#
# **The same reason the greeter beside this file has one, for the other half of
# a machine's morning.** `libseat` asks `logind` which session a process
# belongs to, and a system service belongs to none — so a desktop started by a
# unit is handed no seat and cannot take a card, however correct its drawing is.
#
# `image/usr/lib/pam.d/alo-compositor` records the measurement that found this:
# `After=systemd-logind.service` is ordering and not membership, and the unit
# that waited for logind still got `display session connect failed: Function not
# implemented` — ENOSYS, which from libseat means *no backend at all*. This
# image ships no `seatd` and nothing installs one.
#
# **This authenticates nobody either, and for a different reason from the
# greeter's.** The greeter authenticates nobody because nobody is there yet;
# this authenticates nobody because they already have. `alo-accounts` checked
# the password and `alo-sessiond` opened the session (ADR 0024), and this unit
# only runs at all because that session exists — `BindsTo=user@1000.service`.
# A stack that asked for a password here would ask a person who had just given
# one for it again.
#
# Modelled on `alo-compositor`'s, which is modelled on the base's own
# `systemd-run0`. Kept deliberately identical to the greeter's rather than
# shortened further: the two processes need the same one thing from PAM, and two
# stacks that differ invite a reader to look for a reason they do.

# Nobody is authenticated here; `alo-sessiond` already did, or this unit would
# not be running.
auth       required    pam_permit.so
account    required    pam_permit.so

# SELinux first and last, as every stack in this base does: `close` before the
# session is set up and `open` after, so the process is relabelled into the
# context its unit was given rather than inheriting PID 1's.
session    required    pam_selinux.so close
session    required    pam_selinux.so open

# The audit login uid, so what the desktop does is attributable to the person
# whose desktop it is.
session    required    pam_loginuid.so

# **The line this file exists for.** `required` and not `optional`: a desktop
# without a seat cannot take a card, and a machine that signed somebody in and
# then showed them nothing is the failure this stack is here to end. Named here
# rather than re-emerging two layers down as an errno.
session    required    pam_systemd.so
